<!DOCTYPE en-export SYSTEM "http://xml.evernote.com/pub/evernote-export4.dtd">
<en-export export-date="20261002T000625Z" application="panopto_transcribe.py" version="1.0"><note><title>LIS 467 — Session 1 Review: Client-Server, HTTP &amp; History of the WWW</title><content><![CDATA[<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE en-note SYSTEM "http://xml.evernote.com/pub/enml2.dtd">
<en-note><h1>LIS 467 — Session 1 Review: Client-Server, HTTP &amp; History of the WWW</h1>
<p><strong>Course:</strong> LIS 467 — Web Development &amp; Information Architecture
<strong>Topic:</strong> Review of Session 1 — Client-Server Architecture, HTTP, and the History of the World Wide Web
<strong>Source:</strong> Panopto lecture transcript — "Session 1 review - Client-Server; HTTP; History of WWW"</p>
<hr />
<h2>1. Session Overview</h2>
<p>This session opens by previewing upcoming topics — why web design matters, gathering requirements for a web design project, responsive and mobile-first design, project management, and version control — and then reviews the core concepts from Session 1:</p>
<ul>
<li>Distinguishing between <strong>client</strong> and <strong>server</strong></li>
<li>The <strong>HTTP</strong> request/response cycle</li>
<li>Key developments in the <strong>history of the Internet and the World Wide Web</strong></li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>2. Client vs. Server</h2>
<p>🔑 <strong>Key question:</strong> Can one computer perform both roles?
✅ <strong>Yes.</strong> One computer can act as both a client and a server — you can install server software on your local computer.</p>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Role</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Definition</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Client</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Anything that <em>requests</em> services</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Server</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">A computer that <em>provides</em> services to clients</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Architecture Tiers</h3>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Architecture</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Description</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Two-tier</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Client ↔ Server (simple)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Three-tier</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Client ↔ Application server ↔ Database server (database separated from application logic)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>N-tier</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Multiple separated layers — various applications and services each on their own tier</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>💡 Separating the database and applications into their own tiers is what moves you from two-tier to three-tier (and beyond) architecture.</p>
<hr />
<h2>3. The HTTP Request/Response Cycle</h2>
<p>The typical process:</p>
<ol>
<li>The <strong>end user</strong> acts on the <strong>client</strong> (browser).</li>
<li>The client sends a <strong>request</strong> (e.g., an HTTP <code>GET</code> to fetch something) over the <strong>network</strong> to the <strong>server</strong>.</li>
<li>The server — often with a <strong>database</strong> attached — processes the request.</li>
<li>The server returns a <strong>response</strong> to the client: HTML pages, CSS, JavaScript, and so on.</li>
</ol>
<h3>GET vs. POST</h3>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Method</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Purpose</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Example</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>GET</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Request/retrieve a resource; server returns an acknowledgment (e.g., <code>200 OK</code>) and content</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Fetching a web page</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>POST</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Submit data to the server</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Logging in with a username/password — the server checks credentials against the database to see if you're authorized</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>⚠️ Remember: the connection between client and server is a <em>stateless</em> request/acknowledgment exchange — each request gets its own response.</p>
<hr />
<h2>4. History of the World Wide Web (Timeline: 1989–2019)</h2>
<h3>The Beginning</h3>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Year</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Event</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>1989 (March)</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">⭐ English engineer/computer scientist <strong>Tim Berners-Lee</strong> writes the <em>Information Management: A Proposal</em> — his vision for what would become the World Wide Web</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>1990</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">First version of a search engine conceived; Internet users reach ~2.6 million; Berners-Lee creates <strong>HTTP</strong>, <strong>URLs</strong>, and the <strong>very first browser</strong> (WorldWideWeb)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>1993</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">World Wide Web software released into the <strong>public domain</strong>; early domains registered</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>1994</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Yahoo!</strong> directory service born; <strong>WebCrawler</strong>, the first full-text search engine, launches; first banner ads appear; Internet users reach ~44 million</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>The Browser Wars &amp; Web 1.0</h3>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Year</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Event</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>1995</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Microsoft Internet Explorer</strong> launches (~a year after <strong>Netscape Navigator</strong>) and quickly becomes one of the most popular browsers, displacing Netscape</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>1997</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Pop-up ads appear</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>1998</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Google</strong> search introduced to consumers</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>1999</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Napster</strong> released — peer-to-peer file sharing for music/movies, later shut down over piracy; <strong>Blogger</strong> launches, empowering ordinary people to publish without depending on news media or being computer experts</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>2000</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Internet users ~412 million; the <strong>dot-com bubble bursts</strong>, wiping out many online startup companies</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Web 2.0 and Social Media</h3>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Year</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Event</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>2001</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Wikipedia</strong> launches</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>2002</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Firefox</strong> browser emerges as open-source software</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>2003</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>WordPress</strong> released; <strong>MySpace</strong> launches — a precursor whose ideas eventually lead to Facebook</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>2004</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">⭐ <strong>Facebook</strong> founded (launched publicly Feb 2005 per timeline); <strong>Gmail</strong> era begins</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>2005</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>YouTube</strong> created by three former PayPal employees</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>2006</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Twitter</strong> founded; <strong>Netflix</strong> begins shifting from mailing physical DVDs to online video streaming</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>2007</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">The <strong>#hashtag</strong> becomes common; <strong>iPhone</strong> era begins</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>2008</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>App Store</strong> and <strong>Android Market</strong> launch</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>The Mobile &amp; Billion-User Era</h3>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Year</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Event</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>2010</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Internet users reach ~1.9 billion; <strong>Instagram</strong> founded (Kevin Systrom et al.); <strong>Pinterest</strong> released later that year</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>2012</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Continued platform growth</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>2016</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Internet users ~3.4 billion</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>2018</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">A tough year for <strong>Facebook</strong> — data-privacy scandals and controversies</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>2019</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">The "world record egg" — a simple photo of an egg on Instagram breaks the world record (~18 million likes) within ten days; by 2019+ the photo passed 54 million likes</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>💡 <strong>Big picture:</strong> In 30 years the web went from one engineer's proposal to billions of users, transforming publishing (blogs), commerce (dot-coms, Amazon), media (Netflix, YouTube), and social interaction (Facebook, Twitter, Instagram).</p>
<hr />
<h2>5. Key Terms Glossary</h2>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Term</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Definition</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Client</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Any computer/program that requests services from a server</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Server</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">A computer/program that provides services to clients</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Two-tier architecture</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Direct client ↔ server model</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Three-tier architecture</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Client ↔ application server ↔ database server</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>HTTP</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">HyperText Transfer Protocol — governs request/response between client and server</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>GET</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">HTTP method for requesting/retrieving resources</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>POST</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">HTTP method for submitting data (e.g., login credentials) to the server</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Tim Berners-Lee</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Inventor of the World Wide Web (1989 proposal; created HTTP, URLs, first browser)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Dot-com bubble</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Speculative boom in Internet startups that collapsed around 2000</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Web 2.0</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Era of user-generated content and social platforms (blogs, wikis, social media)</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>6. Quick Review Questions</h2>
<ol>
<li>Can a single computer act as both a client and a server? Explain.</li>
<li>What is the difference between two-tier and three-tier architecture?</li>
<li>Describe what happens between the client and server when a user logs into a website (which HTTP method is used, and what does the server check?).</li>
<li>Who proposed the World Wide Web, in what year, and what three foundational technologies did he create?</li>
<li>Which browser did Internet Explorer displace in the mid-1990s?</li>
<li>What happened to Internet startup companies around the year 2000?</li>
<li>Name two 2004–2006 launches that defined the social media era.</li>
<li>Roughly how many Internet users were there in 1990, 2000, 2010, and 2016?</li>
</ol></en-note>]]></content><created>20261002T000625Z</created><updated>20261002T000625Z</updated><tag>LIS467</tag><tag>lecture-notes</tag><note-attributes><author>panopto_transcribe.py</author><source>markdown</source></note-attributes></note><note><title>LIS 467 — Session 2, Part 1: Why Web Design? Gathering Requirements</title><content><![CDATA[<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE en-note SYSTEM "http://xml.evernote.com/pub/enml2.dtd">
<en-note><h1>LIS 467 — Session 2, Part 1: Why Web Design? Gathering Requirements</h1>
<p><strong>Course:</strong> LIS 467 — Web Development &amp; Information Architecture
<strong>Topic:</strong> Why Web Design, Fundamentals of Good Design, Design Processes &amp; Pre-Design Requirements Gathering
<strong>Source:</strong> Panopto lecture transcript — "Session 2 Part 1 Why Web Design? Gathering Requirements"</p>
<hr />
<h2>1. Learning Objectives</h2>
<p>By the end of this lecture you should be able to:</p>
<ul>
<li>Identify the <strong>reasons for building a website</strong></li>
<li>Discuss the need for <strong>user analysis and requirements gathering</strong></li>
<li>Explain the reasons for going <strong>responsive / mobile-first</strong></li>
<li>Describe the components of <strong>web project management</strong></li>
<li>Understand the role of <strong>version control</strong></li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>2. Why Web Design?</h2>
<p>When you think of web design, many terms come to mind: <strong>aesthetics, interface, HTML/CSS, typography, graphics, content development, information architecture</strong>. Web design and development matter because a website is often the primary face of an organization and the primary channel of service delivery.</p>
<p>Key areas of study for web design:</p>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Area</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">What it covers</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Fundamentals of web design</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">What makes a design "good"</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Website evolution</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">How sites and design generations have changed</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">The design process</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Pre-design work through launch</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Influences on design</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Technology, content, economics</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Accessibility</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Universal web design principles</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Maintenance</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Planning for ongoing improvement</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>💡 <strong>Exercise from lecture:</strong> Browse the web and collect examples of <em>bad</em> and <em>good</em> web design — and articulate <em>why</em> each qualifies. Apply the same analysis to <strong>library websites</strong>.</p>
<hr />
<h2>3. Bad vs. Good Web Design</h2>
<h3>Characteristics of bad websites</h3>
<ul>
<li>⚠️ Difficult to <strong>navigate</strong></li>
<li>⚠️ Components on the page interfere with each other; no aesthetic sense</li>
<li>⚠️ Poor <strong>functionality</strong> — not easy to use from the end user's perspective</li>
<li>⚠️ Missing images, jarring/overly bright color overdoses, cluttered layouts</li>
</ul>
<h3>Characteristics of good websites (example: Zillow.com)</h3>
<ul>
<li>✅ <strong>Clean and simple</strong> — easy to use and usable</li>
<li>✅ Simple, specific wording matched to user goals (buy / rent / sell)</li>
<li>✅ Search front and center; sign-in and key menu items clearly visible</li>
<li>✅ Works equally well on <strong>mobile</strong> — same functionality, adapted layout</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>4. Fundamentals of Good Web Design</h2>
<h3>4.1 Purpose</h3>
<p>⭐ A website must have a <strong>purpose</strong> — not just be a repository for information. The purpose should be tied to the <strong>goals of the organization</strong> (library, archive, business, or a personal site: what do you want to achieve in the next 2–5 years?).</p>
<p>For a library, the primary purpose — <strong>providing services to patrons</strong> — must be front and center in the design.</p>
<p>Typical purposes: to <strong>inform</strong>, to <strong>educate</strong>, to <strong>persuade</strong> (e.g., buy something, join a cause).</p>
<h3>4.2 Influences on design</h3>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Influence</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Examples</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Technology</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Browsers, devices, connection speeds</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Target audience</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Who the site is for</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Designer &amp; content</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Skills available, material to present</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Money / budget</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Resources for design and maintenance</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Time &amp; scope</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Schedule and size of the project</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Media types</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Animation, images, text, video, mixed</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>4.3 Usability</h3>
<p>🔑 <strong>Usability</strong> is a key requirement: the website must be easy to use from the user's perspective — understandable, interesting, and usable in any form. What you see should be what you get; users should not have to puzzle over what to click.</p>
<p>⭐ Classic usability book: <strong>Steve Krug, <em>Don't Make Me Think</em></strong> — a good website never requires the user to stop and think about how to use it.</p>
<h3>4.4 "Rules" are only guidelines</h3>
<p>⚠️ Web design "rules" change over time. Example: pages used to be kept <strong>short</strong> so all content fit on a laptop screen; now, with touch-based <strong>mobile</strong> browsing, long scrolling pages are normal and accepted.</p>
<p>💡 There is <strong>no single right way</strong> to create a website. The right way depends on:
- The <strong>era/conventions</strong> current at the time
- Your <strong>end users</strong> — their comfort with technology
- Their <strong>devices and browsers</strong></p>
<h3>4.5 Give users control (with guidance)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Keep material centered on what users want to see and the information they need.</li>
<li>Don't force one specific path — users should be able to arrive at content in <strong>multiple ways</strong>.</li>
<li>Guide attention so users don't get lost, while preserving a <strong>semblance of control</strong>.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>5. Generations of Web Design</h2>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Generation</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Character</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>1st</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Replacing paper — documents put online</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>2nd</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Flashy elements — spinning/animated gimmicks on every page</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>3rd</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Leading edge — heavy CSS styling, but content took a back seat</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>4th</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">⭐ Content and technology <strong>integrated</strong> — content is the driving element, with deliberate use of <strong>whitespace</strong> in the design</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>💡 Modern design should consciously avoid the problems of the first three generations.</p>
<hr />
<h2>6. Design Methods: Ad Hoc vs. Methodical</h2>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Method</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Description</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">When appropriate</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Ad hoc ("seat of the pants")</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Based on intuition and experience rather than a plan; site thrown together quickly</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Sites with a <strong>short shelf life</strong>; very small sites (a single event, a single class); genuine time emergencies</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Methodical, well-planned</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Includes planning and <strong>quality assurance</strong> — what this course teaches</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Anything intended to last</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Costs of ad hoc design</h3>
<ul>
<li>⚠️ Content gaps ("under construction" pages), outdated content (no time to interview stakeholders)</li>
<li>⚠️ Borrowed/broken scripts and sub-par techniques</li>
<li>⚠️ Poor usability — no time for <strong>task design</strong> or <strong>usability testing</strong></li>
<li>⚠️ Quick fixes accumulate, making the site <strong>difficult to update and maintain</strong></li>
</ul>
<h3>Benefits of methodical design</h3>
<p>✅ A well-thought-out design, tested before launch, saves problems in the long run: more effective, understandable, easy to navigate, and far easier to maintain.</p>
<p>💡 <strong>Reflection prompts from lecture:</strong> What are the top five skills a good web designer/developer needs? Which do you already have? If you could master one technology right away, which would it be? What is your role in a representative design process?</p>
<hr />
<h2>7. Pre-Design Work (Requirements Gathering)</h2>
<h3>7.1 Steps</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Consider the mission</strong> of your organization.</li>
<li><strong>Define the target audience.</strong></li>
<li>⚠️ A common mistake: building a one-size-fits-all website for everyone — it ends up working for no one.</li>
<li>✅ Know your target audience and map the site to that user base; if it also works for others, that's a bonus.</li>
<li><strong>Set goals</strong> — both quick wins and long-term objectives.</li>
<li><strong>Gather content</strong> and <strong>chunk it into logical units.</strong></li>
<li>💡 Analogy: a room full of randomly placed furniture vs. one organized into a seating area, dining area, and desk — logical grouping makes everything usable.</li>
<li>Decide: single page or multiple pages?</li>
<li><strong>Create an outline / plan for your content.</strong></li>
</ol>
<h3>7.2 Design on paper first</h3>
<p>⭐ <strong>The best design happens on paper.</strong> Before touching software, sketch with pencil and paper:</p>
<ul>
<li>The <strong>menu structure</strong></li>
<li>What <strong>pages</strong> the site should contain</li>
<li>How to <strong>label</strong> the parts of the site</li>
<li>How the user's <strong>attention should flow</strong> from one part to another (sequencing)</li>
</ul>
<p>Then produce the actual <strong>sitemap</strong> (PDF/graphic) showing the size and needs of the website and the <strong>hierarchy of pages</strong>, so the development team understands how users will access and how files are organized.</p>
<hr />
<h2>8. Influence of Technology on Design</h2>
<p>Think through your users' technical context:</p>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Factor</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Considerations</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Browser</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Chrome, Firefox, IE/Edge — different market shares and user bases; which standards will you target?</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Operating system</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Windows, macOS, Linux, mobile OSes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Connection speed</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Varies across US states and around the world</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Screen size</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">5-inch phone → laptop → 75-inch television — where is the browsing happening?</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>9. Content Drives Design</h2>
<p>🔑 <strong>Content drives how the website looks.</strong> Different audiences need different site designs:</p>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Audience</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Design emphasis</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Prospective</strong> clients/students</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Impressive, persuasive presentation</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Current</strong> clients/employees</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Functional — quickly finding what they need</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">E-commerce (e.g., Amazon)</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Action-oriented purchase flows</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Government / archives / libraries</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Information-rich, but can still add action items: <em>borrow a book, ask a librarian, listen to Internet radio</em></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Content quality practices</h3>
<ul>
<li>✅ Use <strong>quality content from subject matter experts</strong> — they know best what belongs on the site; talk to them during design.</li>
<li>✅ Have different people <strong>periodically review</strong> the site.</li>
<li>✅ Proofread for <strong>grammar</strong>; have people <em>unfamiliar</em> with the site review it to catch what you missed.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Designer view vs. user view</h3>
<p>⚠️ <strong>University website problem (classic example):</strong> what the institution puts on the homepage vs. what users actually look for:</p>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">What the front page shows</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">What users actually want</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Campus photos / slideshows</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Faculty phone numbers and names</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">News, press releases</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Campus address, application forms</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Letter from the president</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Academic calendar, maps, course listings</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>⭐ <strong>Design from the user's point of view, not the designer's</strong> — not because you have content to showcase, but because users have tasks to accomplish.</p>
<hr />
<h2>10. Economic Considerations</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Budget</strong> for creation and for ongoing <strong>maintenance</strong></li>
<li><strong>Staff time</strong> available (creation + upkeep)</li>
<li><strong>In-house vs. outsourcing</strong> the design work</li>
</ul>
<p>All of these factor into design decisions <em>before</em> you actually begin mapping the site.</p>
<hr />
<h2>11. Key Terms Glossary</h2>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Term</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Definition</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Usability</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">How easy and pleasant a site is to use from the end user's perspective</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Target audience</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">The specific user group a website is designed to serve</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Requirements gathering</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Pre-design work of identifying mission, audience, goals, and content needs</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Ad hoc design</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Quick, intuition-based site creation with no formal plan or QA</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Methodical design</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Planned design process including quality assurance and testing</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Sitemap</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">A diagram of a site's page hierarchy and organization for the development team</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Content chunking</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Organizing content into logical units/groups</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Whitespace</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Deliberate empty space in a design (hallmark of 4th-generation design)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Responsive / mobile-first</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Designing so a site works on mobile devices first, then scales up</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong><em>Don't Make Me Think</em></strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Steve Krug's usability classic: users should never have to puzzle over an interface</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Subject matter expert (SME)</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">The person who knows the content domain best; source of quality content</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>12. Quick Review Questions</h2>
<ol>
<li>Why must a website have a purpose beyond being an information repository, and what should that purpose be tied to?</li>
<li>List four factors that influence web design decisions.</li>
<li>What is the central principle of Steve Krug's <em>Don't Make Me Think</em>?</li>
<li>Why is "webpages should be short" no longer a firm rule?</li>
<li>Describe the four generations of web design. What distinguishes generation 4?</li>
<li>When is ad hoc ("seat of the pants") design acceptable, and what are its risks?</li>
<li>What are the five main pre-design steps?</li>
<li>Why should design start on paper, and what should the paper sketch capture?</li>
<li>What is the classic mismatch on university homepages between designer content and user needs?</li>
<li>Name three economic considerations in web design.</li>
</ol></en-note>]]></content><created>20261002T000625Z</created><updated>20261002T000625Z</updated><tag>LIS467</tag><tag>lecture-notes</tag><note-attributes><author>panopto_transcribe.py</author><source>markdown</source></note-attributes></note><note><title>LIS 467 — Session 2, Part 2: Responsive Web Design &amp; Mobile First</title><content><![CDATA[<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE en-note SYSTEM "http://xml.evernote.com/pub/enml2.dtd">
<en-note><h1>LIS 467 — Session 2, Part 2: Responsive Web Design &amp; Mobile First</h1>
<p><strong>Course:</strong> LIS 467 — Web Development &amp; Information Architecture
<strong>Topic:</strong> Responsive Web Design, the Flexible Canvas, and Mobile-First vs. Desktop-First Design
<strong>Source:</strong> Panopto lecture transcript — "Session 2 Part 2 Responsive Web Design; Mobile First"</p>
<hr />
<h2>1. Learning Objectives</h2>
<ul>
<li>Explain what <strong>responsive design</strong> means and why it exists</li>
<li>Compare <strong>desktop-first (traditional)</strong> and <strong>mobile-first</strong> design approaches</li>
<li>Identify the skills and tools needed to build responsive websites</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>2. The Canvas Analogy — Why Responsive Design?</h2>
<p>💡 <strong>The old days (print):</strong> an artist painted on a <strong>fixed-size canvas</strong> or sheet of paper. Design was easy because you knew exactly the size of the surface you were working on.</p>
<p>⚠️ <strong>The web today:</strong> the "canvas" <em>changes</em>. The screen you design on at home may differ from:</p>
<ul>
<li>Another user's laptop screen size</li>
<li>A large desktop monitor</li>
<li>A <strong>mobile phone</strong> or <strong>tablet</strong></li>
</ul>
<p>🔑 The core challenge of responsive design: <strong>How do you ensure your website looks good in different browsers and at different screen sizes?</strong> Your canvas changes with every device.</p>
<p>⭐ <strong>Ethan Marcotte</strong> coined the term <strong>responsive design</strong>. His argument: the fixed-canvas control designers knew from the <strong>print medium</strong> was simply a function of the <em>limitations of the printed page</em>. The web doesn't have those constraints — so we should <strong>embrace flexibility and design for it</strong>.</p>
<hr />
<h2>3. Skills &amp; Tools for Responsive Design</h2>
<p>💡 <strong>Exercise from lecture:</strong> do some web searching and discuss — how would you describe responsive design, and what skills are required to build a responsive website? Which skills do you need to learn?</p>
<p>Key ingredients mentioned:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>CSS</strong> — especially media queries / flexible layouts</li>
<li><strong>Ready-made templates and frameworks</strong> — existing solutions that help you build responsive websites more easily</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>4. Desktop-First vs. Mobile-First</h2>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Approach</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Process</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Consequence</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Desktop-first (traditional)</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Design on/for a computer screen first, then try to cut content down to fit mobile</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">You start with maximum space and must trim afterward</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Mobile-first</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">⭐ Design for the small mobile screen first, then scale up to tablet, then desktop</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Very little space <strong>forces you to decide what is truly necessary</strong></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Why mobile-first is advocated</h3>
<ul>
<li>✅ With minimal space, you must <strong>focus on the key content</strong> first</li>
<li>✅ Keeps the design aligned with the <strong>goals of the website</strong></li>
<li>✅ Improves <strong>usability</strong> from the end user's point of view</li>
</ul>
<h3>Direction of design</h3>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Strategy</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Order</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Responsive (desktop-first)</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Laptop/desktop → tablet → mobile</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Mobile-first</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Mobile → tablet → desktop</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>5. Key Terms Glossary</h2>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Term</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Definition</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Responsive design</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Designing a website so it adapts and looks good across different browsers, devices, and screen sizes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Ethan Marcotte</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Web designer who coined the term "responsive design"; argued the web should embrace flexibility rather than mimic print's fixed page</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Mobile-first</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Design strategy starting with the smallest screen, forcing prioritization of key content, then scaling up</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Desktop-first</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Traditional strategy: design for large screens first, then reduce for smaller devices</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Fixed canvas</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">The print-era assumption of a known, unchanging design surface — no longer true on the web</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>CSS templates/frameworks</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Ready-made responsive solutions that simplify building adaptable layouts</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>6. Quick Review Questions</h2>
<ol>
<li>Explain the canvas analogy: how does web design differ from print design?</li>
<li>Who coined the term "responsive design," and what was his core argument?</li>
<li>What problem does responsive design solve for users browsing on different devices?</li>
<li>Describe the difference between desktop-first and mobile-first design order.</li>
<li>Why does designing mobile-first force better content decisions?</li>
<li>What two benefits does mobile-first bring to a website's goals and usability?</li>
</ol></en-note>]]></content><created>20261002T000625Z</created><updated>20261002T000625Z</updated><tag>LIS467</tag><tag>lecture-notes</tag><note-attributes><author>panopto_transcribe.py</author><source>markdown</source></note-attributes></note><note><title>LIS 467 — Session 2, Part 3: Project Management</title><content><![CDATA[<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE en-note SYSTEM "http://xml.evernote.com/pub/enml2.dtd">
<en-note><h1>LIS 467 — Session 2, Part 3: Project Management</h1>
<p><strong>Course:</strong> LIS 467 — Web Development &amp; Information Architecture
<strong>Topic:</strong> Project Management for Web Design Projects — Requirements, Scope, the Triple Constraint, Management Areas, Life Cycle &amp; Tracking Tools
<strong>Source:</strong> Panopto lecture transcript — "Session 2 Part 3 Project Management"</p>
<hr />
<h2>1. Context: Your Team as a Web Design Company</h2>
<p>In this class, your team acts as a <strong>web design company</strong> taking on a client website: gathering the requirements, then following the entire process of designing and building the site. Understanding how a <strong>project manager</strong> works — and managing the effort <em>as a project</em> — is essential to delivering on time.</p>
<hr />
<h2>2. What Is a Project?</h2>
<p>🔑 A <strong>project</strong> is both <strong>temporary</strong> and <strong>unique</strong>:</p>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Property</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Meaning</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Temporary</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">A definite <strong>beginning and end</strong> — not an ongoing operation</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Unique</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">A <strong>new, unfamiliar undertaking</strong> producing a distinct product or service</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>3. Requirements Gathering — Getting on the Same Page</h2>
<p>⚠️ <strong>Things can go horribly wrong</strong> when requirements aren't managed. The classic cartoon: how the <em>customer explained it</em>, how the <em>project leader understood it</em>, how the <em>analyst designed it</em>, how the <em>programmer wrote it</em>, how the <em>business consultant described it</em> — all wildly different; barely any documentation; installation fails; the customer is billed for a roller coaster when they wanted a simple swing.</p>
<p>⭐ <strong>Lesson:</strong> Establish a <strong>common ground and a common language</strong> between you and the client so both of you understand what you're agreeing on. That is why it is critical to <strong>document requirements</strong>.</p>
<hr />
<h2>4. Project Success vs. Failure</h2>
<h3>A project succeeds when:</h3>
<ul>
<li>✅ Customer requirements are <strong>satisfied or exceeded</strong> (exceeding is hard — customers often see the work and generate <em>new</em> requirements)</li>
<li>✅ Completed within the <strong>allocated budget</strong></li>
<li>✅ Delivered <strong>on time</strong></li>
<li>✅ <strong>Accepted by the customer</strong></li>
</ul>
<h3>A project fails when there is:</h3>
<ul>
<li>⚠️ <strong>Scope creep</strong> (see below)</li>
<li>⚠️ Unrealistic <strong>planning and scheduling</strong></li>
<li>⚠️ Sloppy <strong>requirements gathering</strong></li>
<li>⚠️ <strong>Lack of resources</strong> to complete the tasks</li>
<li>⚠️ <strong>Lack of skills</strong> required for the task at hand</li>
</ul>
<h3>Scope creep</h3>
<p>🔑 <strong>Scope creep</strong> = uncontrolled changes or continuous growth in the project's scope. It occurs when the scope is <strong>not fully defined, not documented, or not well controlled</strong>, and is generally considered harmful.</p>
<p>✅ <strong>Managing it:</strong> Scope changes are acceptable <em>if</em> budget, resources, and schedule increase along with the scope.</p>
<p>💡 <strong>CIO anecdote (from the instructor's PhD days in Singapore):</strong> when asked "can you add this to the project?", the CIO would always say <strong>yes — but this will cost extra and take additional time.</strong> Requirements can grow, as long as you have a mechanism to manage the growth: more people, more time, or more money.</p>
<hr />
<h2>5. What Is Project Management?</h2>
<p>⭐ <strong>Project management</strong> is the application of <strong>skills, knowledge, tools, and techniques</strong> to meet the needs and expectations of the project's <strong>stakeholders</strong>.</p>
<p>💡 Its purpose is <strong>prediction and prevention</strong>, not reaction — plan well enough to foresee problems and prevent them from happening.</p>
<hr />
<h2>6. The Triple Constraint</h2>
<p>🔑 Project management balances three interlocking constraints. Together they determine the <strong>quality</strong> of the project result:</p>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Constraint</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Meaning</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Class example</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Time</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Deliver within a given schedule</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">The class project must be delivered before the last class of the semester</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Cost</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Money and resources available</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">The budget the client provides</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Scope</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">How much you're required to produce</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">e.g., a 5–8 page or 8–12 page website as agreed</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Trade-offs — change one, adjust the others:</h3>
<ul>
<li>Scope ↑ → time ↑ and cost ↑</li>
<li>Time ↓ (tight deadline) → cost ↑ (hire more people) and/or scope ↓ (agree to drop requirements)</li>
<li>Cost ↓ (tight budget) → ask for more time and/or reduce scope</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>7. Areas of Project Management</h2>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Area</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">What it covers</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Scope management</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Ensuring <em>all</em> the work within scope — and <em>only</em> that work — is completed; defining how scope is set, who manages it, and how changes are vetted</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Issue management</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Handling issues (constraints to accomplishing project deliverables); issues that can't be easily resolved are <strong>escalated</strong>, logged, and <strong>tracked to resolution</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Cost management</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Controlling costs and how they are paid: <strong>resources</strong> (people, equipment, materials), full-time vs. contract staff, office space, estimated vs. actual costs, budget</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Quality management</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Quality assurance plan — testing (usability testing, other agreed testing) to ensure the project <strong>conforms to requirements</strong> and is suitable for use</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Communications management</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Agreeing how/when you report to the client: designated contact person, meeting schedule, email updates</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Risk management</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">All projects should begin with an agreement on how risks will be handled: identify risks, plan <strong>mitigation strategies</strong>, maintain and update a <strong>risk tracking log</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Change control</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Managing changes to scope, specifications, and schedule — documenting and tracking each change</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>8. Project Life Cycle</h2>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Phase</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">What happens</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>1. Initiation / decision</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Deciding to undertake the project</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>2. Definition</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Gathering requirements; defining what the project must deliver</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>3. Planning</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">How to proceed: hire people? learn skills? acquire resources?</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>4. Implementation</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">The actual work happens — with <strong>usability testing at various levels</strong> along the way</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>5. Verification</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Confirm requirements are satisfied and the user signs off that the website is ready</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>6. Closing</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Wrap up the project and move on</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>9. Project Tracking Tools</h2>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Tool</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Description</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Simple activity table</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Rows = activities of the project; columns = time (weeks required per activity, current status)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Gantt chart</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Larger activities broken into sub-activities, showing time required for each and completion status (many free tools online — search "project management tools")</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>PERT chart</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>P</strong>rogram <strong>E</strong>valuation <strong>R</strong>eview <strong>T</strong>echnique — designed to analyze and represent the tasks involved in completing a given project; e.g., a network chart for a 7-month project with 5 milestones (10, 20, 30, 40, 50) and 6 activities, showing the time required for each</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Project schedule</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">The documented timeline agreed with the client</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>10. Key Terms Glossary</h2>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Term</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Definition</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Project</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">A temporary undertaking (definite beginning and end) producing a unique product or service</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Project management</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Applying skills, knowledge, tools, and techniques to meet stakeholder needs and expectations</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Stakeholder</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Anyone with a need or expectation the project must satisfy (client, users, team)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Scope</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">The agreed amount of work/deliverables in the project</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Scope creep</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Uncontrolled, continuous growth of project scope without matching increases in time/budget/resources</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Triple constraint</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Time, cost, and scope — the three interdependent limits that determine quality</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Issue management</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Logging, escalating, and tracking problems that block deliverables</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Risk management</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Identifying risks up front, agreeing on mitigation strategies, and tracking them</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Quality assurance</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Testing processes (incl. usability testing) that ensure conformance to requirements</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Change control</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Documented management of changes to scope, specification, or schedule</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Gantt chart</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Bar-chart timeline of activities, durations, and completion status</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>PERT chart</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Network diagram (Program Evaluation Review Technique) of project tasks and milestones</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Project life cycle</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Initiation → definition → planning → implementation → verification → closing</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>11. Quick Review Questions</h2>
<ol>
<li>What two properties define a <em>project</em>, and what does each mean?</li>
<li>Why is documenting requirements so important? (Think of the "how the customer explained it" cartoon.)</li>
<li>List the four criteria for a successful project.</li>
<li>Define scope creep. When is added scope acceptable?</li>
<li>How did the Singapore CIO respond to new feature requests, and what principle does that illustrate?</li>
<li>What is the purpose of project management — reaction, or something else?</li>
<li>Name the three parts of the triple constraint and describe one trade-off scenario.</li>
<li>What is the difference between issue management and risk management?</li>
<li>What are the six phases of the project life cycle, and in which phase does usability testing occur?</li>
<li>What do Gantt and PERT charts each show?</li>
</ol></en-note>]]></content><created>20261002T000625Z</created><updated>20261002T000625Z</updated><tag>LIS467</tag><tag>lecture-notes</tag><note-attributes><author>panopto_transcribe.py</author><source>markdown</source></note-attributes></note><note><title>LIS 467 — Session 2, Part 4: Web Project Management</title><content><![CDATA[<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE en-note SYSTEM "http://xml.evernote.com/pub/enml2.dtd">
<en-note><h1>LIS 467 — Session 2, Part 4: Web Project Management</h1>
<p><strong>Course:</strong> LIS 467 — Web Development &amp; Information Architecture
<strong>Topic:</strong> Web-Specific Project Management — Requirements, Usability, Personas, Accessibility, Feedback &amp; Maintenance
<strong>Source:</strong> Panopto lecture transcript — "Session 2 Part 4 Web Project Management"</p>
<hr />
<h2>1. General vs. Web Project Management</h2>
<p><strong>General project management</strong> applies to any sphere — construction, software, anything. <strong>Web project management</strong> applies those same disciplines specifically to building websites.</p>
<p>💡 <strong>Reflection prompts from lecture:</strong> Why should a web designer/developer bother with project management? What is involved in managing a web project? What prior project management experience do you have?</p>
<p>⭐ <strong>Real-world example (ASIS&amp;T website redesign):</strong> The instructor served on the board when the Association for Information Science and Technology outsourced its website redesign. The project involved <strong>over $100,000</strong> and the winning team spent <strong>~9 months</strong> gathering requirements — talking to stakeholders about what was working, what wasn't, and what they wanted to see. Web projects for large organizations can be <em>big</em>.</p>
<hr />
<h2>2. Web Project Requirements (Gathered via Interviews)</h2>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Requirement type</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Questions to answer</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Goals &amp; audiences</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">What is the site for, and who uses it? (typically established through interviews)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Content requirements</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">What content already exists? What should be removed? What needs to be added? How should it be structured and sequenced?</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Technical requirements</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">What technologies/CMS will be used?</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Visual requirements</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Logo, color scheme, imagery</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Site structure</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Architecture and construction of the site</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Timeline</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Project schedule and deliverables</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Budget</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Cost estimates</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>3. Usability — Design for People</h2>
<p>🔑 <strong>Make the website people-centric</strong> — design from the point of view of the end user. Usability applies to <em>any</em> technology (even a pen: does it write well? feel right?); for a website it means:</p>
<ul>
<li>Is the <strong>content useful</strong>?</li>
<li>Is the site <strong>navigable</strong>?</li>
<li>Does it serve the end user — and the <em>various categories</em> of users?</li>
<li><strong>How many clicks</strong> does it take to get where you need to go?</li>
</ul>
<h3>Personas</h3>
<p>⭐ Rather than designing for "generic people," <strong>envision a real person</strong> accessing your website — a <strong>persona</strong>: a fictional but detailed typical user. Imagine their age, where they work, their requirements, and <em>why</em> they would visit your site. (Covered in depth in upcoming sessions.)</p>
<h3>Don't make users think or remember</h3>
<ul>
<li>✅ Keep it <strong>simple</strong> — users won't put effort into remembering how your site works.</li>
<li>💡 In the past, software shipped with help menus users were expected to read; modern design (e.g., Apple iOS devices) is <strong>intuitive</strong> — even children who can't read the language learn iPads by tapping. Aim for that.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Use redundant, recognizable conventions</h3>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Convention</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Expected location</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Menus</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Top left (desktop); <strong>hamburger menu</strong> top right (mobile)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Contact info / social media links / address</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Bottom (footer)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">⚠️ Menu at the bottom</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Counterintuitive — avoid</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Links</h3>
<ul>
<li>✅ Show <strong>visited links in a different color</strong> so users remember where they've been</li>
<li>⚠️ Links must <strong>not look the same as plain text</strong> — they should be visibly clickable</li>
</ul>
<h3>Choices and response time</h3>
<ul>
<li>Limit the number of items in groups of choices — don't overload one page.</li>
<li>🔑 <strong>Response time matters.</strong> Tolerance is proportional to perceived payoff: in dial-up days people waited minutes; now even a few seconds feels long.</li>
<li>⚠️ <strong>~10-second rule:</strong> with no feedback within about 10 seconds, users become impatient and may leave.</li>
<li>✅ Provide <strong>progress indicators</strong> (like installer/download progress bars) whenever an operation takes more than a few seconds, so users know how much longer it will take.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>4. Using Cutting-Edge Technologies</h2>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Poor reasons</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Good reasons</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Just to look cool</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">To <strong>inform, educate, engage</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">To prove that you can</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">To draw attention appropriately and enhance information</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Because you have them available</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">E.g., JavaScript for specific behaviors, animation that genuinely enhances the user experience</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>5. Accessibility</h2>
<p>⭐ Accessibility is essential in modern web building — ensure your site meets <strong>basic accessibility requirements</strong>:</p>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Law</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Requirement</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Section 508, Rehabilitation Act (1986/1998)</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Entities doing business with the federal government must include solutions usable by employees with disabilities</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Americans with Disabilities Act (1990)</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Employers (15+ employees) must provide reasonable accommodations</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>💡 Universities must also adhere to accessibility requirements. Later in the semester the course covers accessibility requirements and how to test your site against them (validators/checkers are available online).</p>
<hr />
<h2>6. Feedback, Proofreading &amp; Validation</h2>
<ul>
<li>✅ Get feedback on your design from <strong>other web designers</strong>, <strong>subject matter experts</strong>, and different parties — on both content and design.</li>
<li>✅ <strong>Proofread for grammar.</strong></li>
<li>✅ <strong>Validate</strong> the site for standards compliance (e.g., W3C validator) and run accessibility checks.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>7. Maintenance Planning</h2>
<p>Once the website is done, it must be maintained. Agree up front:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Who</strong> will do the maintenance — will you hand it over to the client, or maintain it periodically yourself?</li>
<li><strong>What happens if major problems occur</strong> — keep <strong>backup copies</strong> of the site (e.g., in case of a virus/attack).</li>
<li><strong>Scheduled periodic review:</strong> Does content need updating? Is the design still working? Are there new tools you should use?</li>
</ul>
<h3>Resources mentioned</h3>
<ul>
<li>Website optimization resources and statistics links</li>
<li><strong>W3C validator</strong>, connection-speed checkers</li>
<li><strong>W3Schools</strong> tutorials</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>8. Key Terms Glossary</h2>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Term</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Definition</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Web project management</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Applying project management skills specifically to website builds</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Persona</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">A detailed fictional profile of a typical end user, used to guide design</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Usability</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">How useful, navigable, and user-serving a site is (incl. click depth)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Hamburger menu</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">The three-line mobile menu icon, conventionally top right</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>10-second rule</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Users become impatient without feedback within ~10 seconds</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Progress indicator</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Visual feedback showing how long an operation will take</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Section 508</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Federal accessibility requirement for entities doing business with the US government</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>ADA (1990)</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Americans with Disabilities Act — reasonable accommodation requirements</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Validation</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Checking a site against standards (W3C) and accessibility guidelines</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Maintenance plan</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Agreement on who maintains the site, backups, and scheduled reviews</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>9. Quick Review Questions</h2>
<ol>
<li>How does web project management differ from general project management?</li>
<li>In the ASIS&amp;T redesign example, roughly how much money and how much requirements-gathering time were involved?</li>
<li>List five categories of requirements gathered for a web project.</li>
<li>What is a persona and why is it better than designing for "generic people"?</li>
<li>Where do users conventionally expect menus and contact information?</li>
<li>Why should visited links be a different color, and how should links differ from plain text?</li>
<li>What is the ~10-second rule, and what should you provide for longer operations?</li>
<li>Give two poor and two good reasons for using cutting-edge web technologies.</li>
<li>Which two US laws drive web accessibility requirements?</li>
<li>Name three questions a maintenance plan must answer.</li>
</ol></en-note>]]></content><created>20261002T000625Z</created><updated>20261002T000625Z</updated><tag>LIS467</tag><tag>lecture-notes</tag><note-attributes><author>panopto_transcribe.py</author><source>markdown</source></note-attributes></note><note><title>LIS 467 — Session 2, Part 5: Version Control (Git &amp; GitHub)</title><content><![CDATA[<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE en-note SYSTEM "http://xml.evernote.com/pub/enml2.dtd">
<en-note><h1>LIS 467 — Session 2, Part 5: Version Control (Git &amp; GitHub)</h1>
<p><strong>Course:</strong> LIS 467 — Web Development &amp; Information Architecture
<strong>Topic:</strong> Why Version Control, How Git Works (Working Directory → Staging → Local Repo → Remote), Core Git Commands
<strong>Source:</strong> Panopto lecture transcript — "Session 2 Part 5 Version Control"</p>
<hr />
<h2>1. The Problem: Sharing Code in a Team</h2>
<p>When working in a team you need to share code (CSS, JavaScript, any language) with teammates. The naive options all fall short:</p>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Method</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Problem</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Email attachments</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">⚠️ No coordination — what if two people edit the same file at the same time?</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>"Sneakernet"</strong> (thumb drives, hard disks, CD-ROMs)</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">⚠️ Physically transporting copies; same conflict problem</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Cloud storage</strong> (Google Docs, Dropbox)</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">⚠️ No <strong>merging</strong> for code, no <strong>syntax highlighting</strong>, not designed for source code</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>2. Git: A Version Control System Designed for Code</h2>
<p>🔑 <strong>Git</strong> is a version control system that lets you:</p>
<ul>
<li>✅ Control your <strong>source code</strong> and its different <strong>versions</strong></li>
<li>✅ <strong>Track all changes</strong> submitted — who changed what, and why</li>
<li>✅ <strong>Revert</strong> changes / go back to previous versions</li>
<li>✅ Create <strong>branches</strong> — work on something separately without disturbing the main code, then <strong>merge</strong> it back if it works</li>
<li>✅ Detect <strong>conflicts</strong> when merging</li>
</ul>
<h3>Why Git specifically?</h3>
<ul>
<li>⭐ It's the <strong>#1 version control system</strong> — nearly every programmer/developer uses it</li>
<li>⭐ If you work in industry, you will work with it</li>
</ul>
<h3>Other version control systems</h3>
<p>GitHub (hosting), <strong>GitLab</strong>, <strong>Beanstalk</strong>, Apache <strong>Subversion</strong>, Microsoft <strong>Team Foundation Server</strong>, <strong>Mercurial</strong>, <strong>CVS</strong> (Concurrent Versions System), <strong>RCS</strong>, <strong>Bitbucket</strong>, etc. Git is covered here because it's the most popular.</p>
<hr />
<h2>3. GitHub: The Remote Repository</h2>
<p>⭐ <strong>github.com</strong> is a hosting service acting as the <strong>remote repository</strong> for your code:</p>
<ul>
<li>Create an account and host your repositories</li>
<li>Browse repositories published by other people</li>
<li><strong>Clone</strong> local copies, modify them, <strong>commit</strong> to your local repository, and <strong>push</strong> back to the remote</li>
<li>One giant "repository of repositories" for all kinds of code</li>
</ul>
<p>💡 <strong>Distributed</strong> design: every user maintains a <strong>local copy of the entire repository</strong> — a mirror of the remote server on your own computer. Multiple people work simultaneously on their own local repositories, committing locally and pushing/pulling to stay in sync.</p>
<hr />
<h2>4. The Four Areas of Git</h2>
<pre><code>flowchart LR
    W[Working directory] -- git add --&gt; S[Staging area / index]
    S -- git commit --&gt; L[Local repository]
    L -- git push --&gt; R[Remote repository - GitHub]
    R -- git pull / fetch --&gt; L
    L -- git checkout --&gt; W
</code></pre>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Area</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">What it is</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Working directory (workspace)</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">The local files you're editing right now — do anything you want here; initially nothing is tracked</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Staging area (index)</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Files "getting ready" to be committed — add files when they're ready; you can also remove files from staging</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Local repository</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Your local copy of the full version history — commits land here with messages</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Remote repository</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">The shared server copy (e.g., GitHub) — push commits up, pull others' work down</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>5. Core Git Commands</h2>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Command</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Purpose</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><code>git init</code></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Initialize a new repository from scratch in the current directory</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><code>git clone</code></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Copy an existing remote repository onto your local computer</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><code>git status</code></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Check the state of the current project</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><code>git add</code></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Move file changes from the working directory to the <strong>staging area</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><code>git commit</code></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Record staged changes into the <strong>local repository</strong> — ⭐ always with a <strong>commit message</strong> describing what you changed, so teammates can see, and so you can later see exactly what changed in every version</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><code>git push</code></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Send local commits to the <strong>remote repository</strong> so the rest of the team can access them</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><code>git pull</code></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Fetch the most up-to-date copy from the server — 🔑 always pull before working so you build on the latest version, not a stale copy</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><code>git fetch</code></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Get updates from the remote into the local repository</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><code>git checkout</code></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Get files from the local repository (or staging) back into your working directory; also switches branches</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><code>git branch &lt;name&gt;</code></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Create a branch — try things out without disturbing existing files; multiple branches can exist; merge back when ready</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><code>git diff</code></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Check differences between checkpoints/versions</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>💡 Connect to remotes over HTTPS or <strong>SSH</strong>. Install Git via the command line (most developers), or GUI clients like <strong>GitHub Desktop</strong> for Windows/Mac; the command-line interface works like standard Linux/UNIX commands.</p>
<hr />
<h2>6. Why It Matters: The "Jenny" Scenario</h2>
<p>⚠️ <strong>Without version control:</strong> Jenny makes an error just before a big release; the bad code ships and the customer is unhappy, with no easy way back.</p>
<p>✅ <strong>With version control:</strong> Jenny pushes her changes, the error is caught in review/merge before release — and even if it ships, the team can <strong>revert</strong> to the previous working version.</p>
<hr />
<h2>7. Key Terms Glossary</h2>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Term</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Definition</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Version control system (VCS)</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Software that tracks changes to files, supports reverting, branching, and merging</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Git</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">The dominant distributed VCS, designed for source code</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>GitHub</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Web hosting service for Git remote repositories</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Repository (repo)</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">The stored project + its full version history</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Working directory</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Your local editable files (untracked until added)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Staging area (index)</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Holding area for changes about to be committed</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Commit</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">A recorded snapshot of staged changes, with a message</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Push / Pull</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Send commits to / retrieve updates from the remote repository</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Clone</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Copy an entire remote repository to your computer</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Branch</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">A separate line of development that doesn't disturb the main code</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Merge</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Combining a branch's changes back in (Git flags conflicts)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Sneakernet</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Physically carrying files on drives/disks — the pre-VCS "solution"</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>8. Quick Review Questions</h2>
<ol>
<li>Why are email, sneakernet, and Dropbox/Google Docs inadequate for sharing code?</li>
<li>What four capabilities does Git give you that those methods lack?</li>
<li>What is the relationship between Git and GitHub?</li>
<li>Name the four areas of Git and the commands that move changes between them.</li>
<li>Why should every commit include a message?</li>
<li>Why should you <code>git pull</code> before starting work?</li>
<li>What is a branch for, and what happens when you merge?</li>
<li>Name three version control systems other than Git.</li>
<li>In the "Jenny" scenario, how does version control prevent (or recover from) a bad release?</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<p><em>This concludes Session 2: why web design, requirements gathering, responsive/mobile-first design, project management, and version control.</em></p></en-note>]]></content><created>20261002T000625Z</created><updated>20261002T000625Z</updated><tag>LIS467</tag><tag>lecture-notes</tag><note-attributes><author>panopto_transcribe.py</author><source>markdown</source></note-attributes></note><note><title>LIS 467 — Session 2 Review: Reasons, Requirements, Mobile First, Project Management, GitHub</title><content><![CDATA[<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE en-note SYSTEM "http://xml.evernote.com/pub/enml2.dtd">
<en-note><h1>LIS 467 — Session 2 Review: Reasons, Requirements, Mobile First, Project Management, GitHub</h1>
<p><strong>Course:</strong> LIS 467 — Web Development &amp; Information Architecture
<strong>Topic:</strong> Recap of Session 2 (reasons for web design, requirements, responsive/mobile-first design, project management, Git/GitHub) and preview of Session 3
<strong>Source:</strong> Panopto lecture transcript — "Session 2 review - reasons, requirements, mobile first, project mgmt, github"</p>
<hr />
<h2>1. Session 3 Preview</h2>
<p>This session covers:</p>
<ul>
<li>⭐ The importance of understanding and describing your <strong>audience</strong>, and building <strong>personas</strong></li>
<li><strong>Design considerations</strong> for websites, with examples of <strong>good and not-so-good</strong> websites</li>
<li>The full suite of <strong>web development tools</strong> you need to know</li>
<li>Starting to learn <strong>HTML5</strong></li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>2. Recap: What Session 2 Covered</h2>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Session 2 Topic</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Key Idea</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Reasons for designing a website</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Why we build sites at all and what a web designer/developer needs to know</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Requirements</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Gathering what the site must do before building</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Desktop-first vs. mobile-first</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Designing for large screens vs. small screens first</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Project management</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Phases of a project; triple constraint</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>GitHub &amp; version control</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Managing versions of code across a team</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Discussion questions from Session 2</h3>
<ul>
<li>What are the <strong>top five skills</strong> you need to be a good web designer/developer? Which do you have, which do you wish to acquire, and what piece of technology would you learn right away?</li>
<li>How would you describe <strong>responsive design</strong>? What skills are needed to make a website responsive?</li>
<li>Is it better to design <strong>desktop-first</strong> or <strong>mobile-first</strong>? Why?</li>
<li>What is your experience with <strong>project management</strong>? What is involved in managing a project?</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>3. Technology Influences on Design</h2>
<p>Different users experience your site through very different technology, so you must know <strong>which user group</strong> you are designing for.</p>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Factor</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Variation</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Browsers</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Different users use different browsers — know which ones your audience uses</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Operating systems</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Desktop: Windows, macOS, Linux · Mobile: iOS, Android</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Connection speeds</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Vary by state, region, and country</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Screen sizes</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Many phone sizes, tablets, laptops of different sizes, desktops</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>4. Desktop-First vs. Mobile-First (Recap)</h2>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Approach</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">How it works</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Desktop-first</strong> (graceful degradation)</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Design for the <strong>large screen</strong> first, then decide what to <strong>cut</strong> for smaller screens</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Mobile-first</strong> (progressive enhancement)</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Design for the <strong>minimum</strong> first, then decide what to <strong>add</strong> as space increases</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>💡 <strong>Decluttering-a-house analogy:</strong>
- One way to declutter is to ask <em>"what should I throw out?"</em> — hard, because you find yourself wanting to keep almost everything.
- The other way is to ask <em>"what should I keep?"</em> — you start with nothing and choose only what you really want.
- ⭐ Mobile-first is like the second approach: it <strong>forces you to think carefully about what is really necessary</strong> versus what is not.</p>
<hr />
<h2>5. Project Management Recap: The Triple Constraint</h2>
<p>Quality in any project depends on balancing <strong>time</strong>, <strong>cost</strong>, and <strong>scope</strong>. Changing one affects the others:</p>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">If…</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Then…</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Scope increases</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">You need <strong>more time</strong> and probably <strong>more cost</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Time gets shorter</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Costs increase</strong> and/or <strong>scope</strong> may need to be reduced</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Budget is tight</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">You may need <strong>more time</strong> (can't hire many people) and/or must <strong>reduce scope</strong></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Planning considerations for a web project</h3>
<p>Before building, consider:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Audience</strong> — who the site is for and what content goes into it</li>
<li><strong>Technical requirements</strong> — what technology is needed</li>
<li><strong>Site structure</strong> — what the site will look like structurally</li>
<li><strong>Team</strong> — who is on it and what experience they need</li>
<li><strong>Timeline</strong> — a few days, a few months, or a year or more?</li>
<li><strong>Budget</strong></li>
</ul>
<p>⚠️ Small websites may not need much time for each step, but <strong>large websites require all of these to be thought through</strong>.</p>
<hr />
<h2>6. Git &amp; GitHub Recap</h2>
<pre><code> LOCAL COMPUTER                                        REMOTE SERVER
 ┌─────────────────┐  add  ┌──────────┐ commit ┌────────────┐ push  ┌──────────┐
 │ Working directory│ ───▶ │ Staging  │ ─────▶ │ Local repo │ ────▶ │ GitHub   │
 └─────────────────┘       │ area     │        └────────────┘ ◀──── │ (remote) │
                           └──────────┘                       pull  └──────────┘
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li><strong>Working directory</strong> — where you edit files</li>
<li><strong>Staging area</strong> — where you put the files you want to commit</li>
<li><strong>Local repository</strong> — your committed history</li>
<li><strong>Remote (GitHub.com)</strong> — shared copy; you <strong>push</strong> to it and <strong>pull</strong> from it</li>
</ul>
<p>⭐ <strong>Team workflow:</strong> one person pushes changes; before another person makes changes, they <strong>pull first</strong> to get the latest version, make their changes, then push. This lets multiple people work on the same website/software <strong>without overwriting each other</strong> and preserves the integrity of the final code.</p>
<p>💡 Git is one of many version control systems, but it's the most widely used.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Key Terms Glossary</h2>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Term</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Definition</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Responsive design</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Design whose layout adapts to different screen sizes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Mobile-first</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Designing for the smallest screen first, then adding features for larger screens</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Desktop-first</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Designing for large screens first, then removing features for smaller screens</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Triple constraint</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">The interdependence of time, cost, and scope in a project</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Scope</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">The features and work a project includes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Working directory</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Local folder where files are edited</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Staging area</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Holding area for changes about to be committed</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Local repository</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Committed version history on your computer</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Push / Pull</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Send commits to / fetch commits from a remote repository</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>Quick Review Questions</h2>
<ol>
<li>Name four technology factors that vary across users and influence design.</li>
<li>How does the "what to keep" decluttering analogy explain mobile-first design?</li>
<li>If a project's scope increases, what happens to time and cost?</li>
<li>What planning considerations should be addressed before building a large website?</li>
<li>Describe the path a file takes from the working directory to GitHub.</li>
<li>Why should you pull before making changes in a team repository?</li>
</ol></en-note>]]></content><created>20261002T000625Z</created><updated>20261002T000625Z</updated><tag>LIS467</tag><tag>lecture-notes</tag><note-attributes><author>panopto_transcribe.py</author><source>markdown</source></note-attributes></note><note><title>LIS 467 — Session 3, Part 1: Audience &amp; Design Considerations</title><content><![CDATA[<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE en-note SYSTEM "http://xml.evernote.com/pub/enml2.dtd">
<en-note><h1>LIS 467 — Session 3, Part 1: Audience &amp; Design Considerations</h1>
<p><strong>Course:</strong> LIS 467 — Web Development &amp; Information Architecture
<strong>Topic:</strong> Defining the primary audience, the web coordinator role, good vs. bad library websites, evaluating sites with the CRAAP test, usable library websites
<strong>Source:</strong> Panopto lecture transcript — "Session 3 Part 1 - Audience; Design Considerations"</p>
<hr />
<h2>1. Session 3 Objectives</h2>
<ol>
<li>⭐ <strong>Define the primary audience</strong> for your website</li>
<li>Learn to <strong>create a persona</strong></li>
<li>Start getting familiar with <strong>HTML5</strong></li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>2. Defining Your Audience</h2>
<p>🔑 The first question to ask about any website: <strong>"Who is this website for?"</strong></p>
<p>In a library context, the audience might be:</p>
<ul>
<li>Library <strong>staff</strong></li>
<li>Library <strong>patrons</strong></li>
<li>The broader <strong>community</strong></li>
</ul>
<p>⭐ <strong>The audience is the foundation element for everything you decide about your website.</strong></p>
<h3>Possible audience segments</h3>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Segment</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Example</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Language</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">English speakers; people with English as a second language; speakers of other native languages</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Age</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Children, teens, adults</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Role</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Prospective students, current students, faculty, job seekers</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>💡 <strong>You cannot serve everyone</strong> — but if you build a site well for a specific audience, chances are that <strong>other people will also be served well</strong>.</p>
<hr />
<h2>3. What Users Need vs. What Sites Show</h2>
<p>The lecture shows a well-known cartoon comparing a <strong>university website's front page</strong> with what users actually look for:</p>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">What's on the front page</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">What users actually want</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Marketing material, promotional photos, messages from leadership</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Campus address, how to contact a faculty member, job application forms, course info, etc.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<ul>
<li>Marketing content may suit <strong>prospective students</strong>, but <strong>current students</strong> have very different needs.</li>
<li>✅ Solution: provide <strong>audience-based tabs or paths</strong> at the top (e.g., <em>Prospective Students</em>, <em>Current Students</em>, <em>Visitors</em>). Selecting one leads to a page tailored to that audience.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>4. The Web Coordinator Role in Libraries</h2>
<p>A quoted source on <strong>library web management</strong> describes the division of responsibility:</p>
<ul>
<li>The <strong>web librarian / assistant</strong> may be in charge of <em>maintaining</em> the website — updating pages and adhering to standards — but <strong>not the content</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Content</strong> is often managed by individual <strong>library departments</strong>, frequently without coordinated direction.</li>
<li>⭐ Therefore a <strong>web coordinator</strong> is needed who can work with stakeholders across the library to create a <strong>cohesive site structure and content</strong>.</li>
</ul>
<p>🔑 Deciding <em>what</em> content belongs on the site depends on <strong>understanding who the audience is</strong>.</p>
<hr />
<h2>5. Design Considerations: Good vs. Bad Library Websites</h2>
<h3>⚠️ Examples of poor design</h3>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Site</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Problem</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">A memorial library website</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Too busy</strong> — too many items competing for attention at once</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">A public library website</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Looks very dated</strong> — "like a website from the nineties"</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>✅ Examples of good design</h3>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Site</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">What it does well</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Marin County Free Library</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Clean and neat; <strong>audience tabs</strong> (Kids, Teens, etc.) leading to age-specific pages with story times and events</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Boston Public Library</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Find books by <strong>format</strong>; <strong>Services for…</strong> menu by audience — English learners, job seekers, seniors, <strong>users with disabilities</strong> (accessible resources and services)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Phoenix Public Library</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Pages for <strong>Kids</strong> and <strong>Teens</strong>; services overview and event boxes linking to more info. The top area is a little busy, but the rest is fine</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>💡 Good library sites <strong>segment by audience</strong> so each group quickly finds what is relevant to them.</p>
<hr />
<h2>6. Evaluating Websites: The CRAAP Test</h2>
<p>🔑 The <strong>CRAAP test</strong> is a set of criteria for evaluating whether a website is good, bad, or credible:</p>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Letter</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Criterion</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Questions to ask</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>C</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Currency</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">When was it created? When was it last updated? Is that date shown?</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>R</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Relevance</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Who is the intended audience? Is the content relevant to your need?</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>A</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Authority</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Who is the author? Are they associated with a credible organization? Is author/contact information available?</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>A</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Accuracy</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Is the information supported by sources? Do other reliable sites confirm it?</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>P</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Purpose</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Why does the site exist — to inform, sell, persuade, entertain, or deceive?</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Worked example (hoax-style site)</h3>
<p>The lecture walks through an evaluation of a sample site:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Currency:</strong> created 1998, updated in later years — dates are displayed ✅</li>
<li><strong>Relevance:</strong> audience appears to be people interested in preserving endangered species; but the site also runs an online store selling merchandise ⚠️</li>
<li><strong>Authority:</strong> the author's name links to a personal website; the site is <strong>not associated</strong> with a school, educational, or recognized organization ⚠️</li>
<li><strong>Accuracy:</strong> most sources on the subject point back to the author's own blog or similar sites; independent sources say the opposite ⚠️</li>
</ul>
<p>⚠️ Another example site uses <strong>Flash</strong> — not a good idea, as many browsers discourage or no longer support Flash.</p>
<p>💡 Apply the same CRAAP criteria to any website you evaluate.</p>
<hr />
<h2>7. Good Websites for Teaching &amp; Learning</h2>
<p>A yearly list of <strong>Best Websites for Teaching &amp; Learning</strong> (2019 list shown) highlights useful tools, for example:</p>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Site</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Purpose</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Anchor</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Free, easy tools for creating high-quality <strong>podcasts</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Brush Ninja</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Free <strong>animation</strong> app for making animated GIFs</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">…and many more</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Presentation, collaboration, and other teaching/learning tools</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>8. Usable Library Websites</h2>
<p>🔑 <strong>Usability</strong> = the site is <strong>easy to use</strong> and meets users' needs in various ways (covered in more depth in later lectures).</p>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Example</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Why it's usable</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">A <strong>college library</strong> site (instructor's favorite)</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Very <strong>clean</strong>; a menu icon reveals more detail; visit/plan options; <strong>feels like the physical library</strong> — "an extension of the physical space"; hours and contact info visible; <strong>not too much text at once</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Garfield County Libraries</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Clear, sensible structure</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Montana State University Library</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">More information, but still a prominent <strong>search</strong> bar and clear steps/services for users</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Queens Public Library</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Programs and services organized for different audiences</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>⭐ Common pattern: <strong>clean layout, prominent search, audience-specific paths, limited text per screen.</strong></p>
<hr />
<h2>Key Terms Glossary</h2>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Term</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Definition</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Primary audience</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">The main group a website is designed to serve</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Audience segmentation</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Dividing users into groups (age, role, language) with distinct needs</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Web coordinator</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Person who coordinates site structure and content across departments</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>CRAAP test</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Currency, Relevance, Authority, Accuracy, Purpose — website evaluation criteria</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Usability</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">How easy a site is to use and how well it meets users' needs</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Flash</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Obsolete browser plug-in technology; discouraged on modern sites</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>Quick Review Questions</h2>
<ol>
<li>Why is the audience called the "foundation element" of a website?</li>
<li>What does the university-website cartoon illustrate about user needs?</li>
<li>Why might a library need a web coordinator in addition to a web librarian?</li>
<li>What design features make the Marin County and Boston Public Library sites effective?</li>
<li>List and explain the five CRAAP criteria.</li>
<li>What characteristics make a library website "usable"?</li>
</ol></en-note>]]></content><created>20261002T000625Z</created><updated>20261002T000625Z</updated><tag>LIS467</tag><tag>lecture-notes</tag><note-attributes><author>panopto_transcribe.py</author><source>markdown</source></note-attributes></note><note><title>LIS 467 — Session 3, Part 2: Design Considerations &amp; Personas</title><content><![CDATA[<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE en-note SYSTEM "http://xml.evernote.com/pub/enml2.dtd">
<en-note><h1>LIS 467 — Session 3, Part 2: Design Considerations &amp; Personas</h1>
<p><strong>Course:</strong> LIS 467 — Web Development &amp; Information Architecture
<strong>Topic:</strong> Elements of user experience (planes of web design), UX qualities (useful, usable, findable, accessible, desirable, valuable, credible), building personas
<strong>Source:</strong> Panopto lecture transcript — "Session 3 Part 2 - Design Considerations; Personas"</p>
<hr />
<h2>1. The Elements of User Experience</h2>
<p>The lecture presents a well-known chart of the <strong>levels of web design</strong> (the <em>Elements of User Experience</em> model). The web can be viewed two ways:</p>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Perspective</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">View of the web</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Web as software interface</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Focus on <strong>tasks</strong> and <strong>functionality</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Web as hypertext system</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Focus on <strong>information</strong> and <strong>content</strong></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The planes move from <strong>abstract → concrete</strong> (bottom to top) and from <strong>conception → completion</strong> of the project:</p>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Plane</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Software interface side</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Hypertext / information side</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Surface</strong> (most concrete)</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Visual design</strong> — look and feel, treatment of graphics and interface elements</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Visual design</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Skeleton</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Interface design</strong> — interface elements that enable functionality</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Navigation design</strong> — how users move through information · <strong>Information design</strong> — presenting information to facilitate understanding</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Structure</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Interaction design</strong> — how users interact with parts of the site (e.g., useful micro-animations)</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Information architecture</strong> — how the site is structured; how items are organized, named, and labeled</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Scope</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Functional specifications</strong> — detailed description of the functionality the site must include to meet user needs</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Content requirements</strong> — what content must be on the site</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Strategy</strong> (most abstract)</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Site objectives</strong> — business, creative, or other goals · <strong>User needs</strong> — learned via user research, interviews, ethnography, etc.</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Same</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>⭐ Every website design must consider <strong>user needs</strong>, <strong>site objectives</strong>, <strong>content</strong>, <strong>architecture/structure</strong>, and <strong>look and feel</strong>. As you progress, you move up the planes — from design into development.</p>
<hr />
<h2>2. Qualities of a Good Website (UX Honeycomb)</h2>
<p>A site you design must be:</p>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Quality</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Meaning</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">⭐ <strong>Useful</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Serves a real purpose for the user — even entertainment is a purpose. A beautiful site that isn't useful won't get return visits</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Usable</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Easy to use</strong>; makes <strong>intuitive sense</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Findable</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Users can <strong>find the information</strong> on the site easily</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Accessible</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Meets the needs of <strong>people with disabilities</strong> — more than a minimal segment of the population</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Desirable</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Users appreciate and are drawn to the content and design</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Valuable</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Content is worth something to users (e.g., the <em>Best Websites for Teaching &amp; Learning</em> examples)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Credible</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Users <strong>believe</strong> what is on the site</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>💡 <strong>Universal design:</strong> when you meet the needs of people with different disabilities, you also serve <strong>many other people</strong>. Universally designed websites work for a lot of people.</p>
<h3>Web credibility</h3>
<p>🔑 The <strong>Stanford Web Credibility Research</strong> project studies what causes people to believe (or not believe) a website — including design factors, assessment strategies, and what makes sites seem credible or not. A good resource if you're interested in the credibility side of design.</p>
<hr />
<h2>3. Personas</h2>
<p>🔑 A <strong>persona</strong> is an <strong>imaginary but representative person</strong> who stands for a typical member of your audience.</p>
<ul>
<li>Rather than saying "my audience is undergrad students" or "grad students," a persona lets you go <strong>much deeper</strong> into what that person needs.</li>
<li>Instead of "one size fits all," you tailor content to <strong>segments of people</strong>, each represented by a persona.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Real-world audience segmentation examples</h3>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Site</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Segmentation</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Simmons University</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Top tabs for prospective undergrads, graduate students, current students, adult learners, veterans, etc. — each gets a different view</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>New York Public Library</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><em>Books, Music &amp; Movies</em> → new arrivals filtered for <strong>children</strong>, <strong>young adults</strong>, etc.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>User groups might include: male/female/transgender users, single parents, graduate students, PhD candidates, retirees, etc.</p>
<hr />
<h2>4. What Goes Into a Persona</h2>
<p>A persona gives a <strong>detailed description of one representative person from each segment</strong> of site visitors.</p>
<h3>Demographic profile</h3>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Element</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Examples</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Name</strong> &amp; (often) <strong>photo</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">"Joe," with a picture</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Demographics</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Age, country, gender, marital status</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Occupation / job title</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Web manager, student, faculty</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Education &amp; language skills</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Degree level, languages spoken</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Technology &amp; experience</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Devices used; beginner / intermediate / expert web user</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Quote</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">A line summing up what matters most to them</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Needs and goals</h3>
<ul>
<li>What are their <strong>biggest problems</strong> they're trying to solve?</li>
<li>What <strong>needs</strong> do they have most?</li>
<li>What information are they typically <strong>searching for</strong>?</li>
<li>What <strong>goals and tasks</strong> do they have in relation to your site?</li>
</ul>
<h3>Behavior-based profile</h3>
<ul>
<li>What do they do <strong>online</strong>? Active on Twitter? Which <strong>social networks</strong> and systems do they use?</li>
<li>What kinds of information do they <strong>share</strong> online?</li>
<li>What part of the site would they spend the <strong>most time</strong> on?</li>
</ul>
<p>💡 You may create <strong>more than one persona</strong> — one per key segment of your target audience.</p>
<hr />
<h2>5. Why Personas Help</h2>
<p>Personas (per Usability.gov):</p>
<ul>
<li>✅ Express and <strong>focus on major needs</strong> of the most important user groups</li>
<li>✅ Give a <strong>clear picture of user expectations</strong></li>
<li>✅ Help <strong>prioritize</strong> features and content</li>
<li>⭐ Ensure you truly <strong>understand your audience</strong> by spending time building a realistic picture of them</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>6. Best Practices for Developing Personas</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Conduct user research</strong> to answer:</li>
<li>Who are your users and <strong>why</strong> are they using the system?</li>
<li>What <strong>behaviors, assumptions, and expectations</strong> do they have?</li>
<li><strong>Condense</strong> the research, look for <strong>themes</strong>, and refine.</li>
<li>Make personas <strong>as realistic as possible</strong>.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Example persona questions</h3>
<ul>
<li>How did they first discover the site? Why did they decide to use it?</li>
<li>Age, education, profession, experience, <strong>technical experience</strong></li>
<li><strong>Motivation</strong> and personality</li>
<li>Group/role (web manager, student, faculty)</li>
<li>Physical, social, and technological <strong>environment</strong></li>
</ul>
<h3>User research techniques (covered more later)</h3>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Technique</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Purpose</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Focus groups</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Group discussion about needs and reactions</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Individual interviews</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">In-depth understanding of one user</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Surveys</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Broad data from many users</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Card sorting</strong> (information architecture)</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">How users group and label content</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>Key Terms Glossary</h2>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Term</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Definition</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Elements of User Experience</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Five planes: strategy, scope, structure, skeleton, surface</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Functional specifications</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Detailed description of features a site must include</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Content requirements</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Description of the content a site must contain</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Interaction design</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Design of how users interact with the system</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Information architecture (IA)</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Structuring, organizing, and labeling site content</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Information design</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Presenting information to facilitate understanding</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Navigation design</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Designing how users move through the site</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Visual design</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">The look and feel (surface) of the site</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Universal design</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Designing so the product works for as many people as possible</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Persona</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">A fictional, research-based representative user</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Web credibility</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">The degree to which users believe a site's content</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>Quick Review Questions</h2>
<ol>
<li>What are the two perspectives on the web in the Elements of UX model?</li>
<li>Name the five planes from abstract to concrete and one element of each.</li>
<li>List the seven qualities a website should have.</li>
<li>How does universal design benefit users without disabilities?</li>
<li>What is a persona, and how is it better than "my audience is students"?</li>
<li>What elements belong in a persona's demographic and behavioral profile?</li>
<li>Name three user research techniques used to build personas.</li>
</ol></en-note>]]></content><created>20261002T000625Z</created><updated>20261002T000625Z</updated><tag>LIS467</tag><tag>lecture-notes</tag><note-attributes><author>panopto_transcribe.py</author><source>markdown</source></note-attributes></note><note><title>LIS 467 — Session 3, Part 3: Web Development Tools</title><content><![CDATA[<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE en-note SYSTEM "http://xml.evernote.com/pub/enml2.dtd">
<en-note><h1>LIS 467 — Session 3, Part 3: Web Development Tools</h1>
<p><strong>Course:</strong> LIS 467 — Web Development &amp; Information Architecture
<strong>Topic:</strong> HTML/CSS/JavaScript roles, editors, browser developer tools, text editors, image editors, web developer roadmaps, front-end vs. back-end vs. full stack
<strong>Source:</strong> Panopto lecture transcript — "Session 3 Part 3 Web development tools"</p>
<hr />
<h2>1. The Core Front-End Languages</h2>
<p>⭐ Understanding the tools is important as the course moves into <strong>HTML, CSS, and JavaScript</strong>.</p>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Language</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Role</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Example</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>HTML</strong> (HyperText Markup Language; current version <strong>HTML5</strong>)</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Content / structure</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Headings, paragraphs, images, links</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>CSS</strong> (Cascading Style Sheets)</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Presentation</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Colors, fonts, positioning, layout</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>JavaScript</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Behavior</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Hover over an image → text appears; dynamic changes</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>🔑 A key principle (covered later): <strong>separate content (HTML) from presentation (CSS)</strong> and <strong>behavior (JavaScript)</strong>.</p>
<hr />
<h2>2. Categories of Tools</h2>
<h3>2.1 WYSIWYG editors</h3>
<ul>
<li>e.g., <strong>Adobe Dreamweaver</strong> and similar</li>
<li>Offer a <strong>"What You See Is What You Get"</strong> interface — looks like Microsoft Word — alongside the code view</li>
</ul>
<h3>2.2 Browser extensions &amp; validators</h3>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Tool</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Purpose</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Web Developer toolbar</strong> (Firefox/Chrome extension)</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Inspect and manipulate parts of a page</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>W3C Markup Validator</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Check HTML against standards</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Browser developer tools</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Inspect elements, CSS, console, device emulation</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>2.3 Chrome Developer Tools (demo)</h3>
<p>Open via <strong>⋮ menu → More tools → Developer tools</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>✅ <strong>Device toolbar</strong> — see how a page looks on a <strong>mobile screen</strong></li>
<li>✅ <strong>Elements</strong> panel — view the HTML; select an element to see what's connected to it; expand/collapse nested elements</li>
<li>✅ <strong>Styles</strong> — the CSS applied to the selected element</li>
<li>✅ <strong>Console</strong> — JavaScript messages/errors</li>
<li>✅ Other info: file types loaded, memory use, etc.</li>
</ul>
<p>Chrome Web Store → search <strong>"web development"</strong> for extensions: Web Developer toolbar, <strong>mobile responsive</strong> testers, eye-trackers/color tools, and other design utilities.</p>
<p>💡 Whatever browser you use, there are developer tools to help you inspect and understand websites easily.</p>
<h3>2.4 Text editors</h3>
<p>You need a <strong>text editor</strong> to write code:</p>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Platform / Editor</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Notepad++</strong> (Windows)</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Free; color-codes code by language</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>TextEdit / BBEdit</strong> (Mac)</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Built-in / lightweight options</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Sublime Text</strong>, <strong>Brackets</strong>, <strong>Komodo Edit</strong>, etc.</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Cross-platform code editors</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Notepad++ demo:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Copy sample HTML (e.g., from <strong>W3Schools</strong>) into a new file.</li>
<li>Before saving, Notepad++ doesn't know the file type — <strong>no color coding</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Save as <code>.html</code></strong> → syntax highlighting is applied automatically (tags turn blue, etc.), and you can <strong>collapse/expand</strong> opening and closing tags.</li>
</ol>
<p>⭐ Syntax highlighting makes code much easier to read and debug.</p>
<h3>2.5 Image editors</h3>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Tool</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>GIMP</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">✅ <strong>Free and open source</strong> image editor — crop, resize, apply filters/effects</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Adobe Photoshop</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Commercial alternative</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>3. The Web Developer Landscape (Roadmaps)</h2>
<p>The lecture shows several articles/roadmaps (2018, 2019, <strong>2020 Web Developer Roadmap</strong>) listing what a web developer could learn. ⚠️ <strong>Not all of this is covered in the course</strong> — it's an overview of what exists.</p>
<h3>Typical roadmap sections</h3>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Area</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Examples</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Introduction / core competencies</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Concepts, languages, data, intermediate skills, <strong>soft skills</strong>, glossary</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Languages</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>JavaScript</strong>; libraries like <strong>jQuery</strong> (covered in this course); <strong>PHP</strong>; others</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Data</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Database options; <strong>SQL</strong> (standard query language); NoSQL</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Frameworks</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Front-end: <strong>React</strong>, <strong>Angular</strong>, <strong>Vue</strong>; back-end frameworks</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Utilities</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Package managers, build tools</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Version control</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Git / GitHub</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Deployment</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">How to deploy code; hosting platforms</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Front-end roadmap essentials (what this course focuses on)</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>HTML:</strong> basics, <strong>semantic HTML</strong>, <strong>SEO</strong> (search engine optimization), <strong>accessibility</strong></li>
<li><strong>CSS:</strong> basics, <strong>layouts</strong>, <strong>media queries</strong>, <strong>responsive</strong> pages, positioning elements</li>
<li><strong>JavaScript:</strong> syntax basics, manipulating the <strong>DOM</strong> (Document Object Model)</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>4. Front-End vs. Back-End vs. Full Stack</h2>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Term</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Meaning</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Front-end (client-side)</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">What runs in the browser — HTML, CSS, JavaScript; design and development of the user-facing site</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Back-end (server-side)</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Server logic, databases, APIs</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Full stack</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">A developer who can program <strong>both</strong> front-end and back-end</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>⭐ <strong>This course focuses on the front-end / client side.</strong> Server-side development is not covered in depth.</p>
<hr />
<h2>5. Takeaways</h2>
<ul>
<li>💡 Web development is a <strong>learning journey</strong> — you pick up more over time.</li>
<li>✅ For this class, choose <strong>one or two tools in each category</strong> (editor, browser tools, image editor, validator) to support your front-end design and development process.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Key Terms Glossary</h2>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Term</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Definition</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>HTML5</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Current version of HyperText Markup Language — defines content/structure</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>CSS</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Cascading Style Sheets — controls presentation and layout</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>JavaScript</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Scripting language that adds behavior/interactivity</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>WYSIWYG</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">"What You See Is What You Get" visual editor</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Developer tools</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Browser-built tools for inspecting HTML, CSS, JS, and device views</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Validator</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Tool that checks code against standards (e.g., W3C)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Syntax highlighting</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Color-coding of code by language element</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>GIMP</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Free, open-source image editor</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>jQuery</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">JavaScript library simplifying DOM manipulation</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Framework</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Pre-built structure for building large apps (React, Angular, Vue)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>DOM</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Document Object Model — the browser's tree representation of a page</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Front-end / Back-end / Full stack</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Client-side / server-side / both</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>Quick Review Questions</h2>
<ol>
<li>What are the roles of HTML, CSS, and JavaScript?</li>
<li>How do you open Chrome Developer Tools, and what can you do with them?</li>
<li>Why does Notepad++ only color-code after saving as <code>.html</code>?</li>
<li>What free tool can replace Photoshop for basic image editing?</li>
<li>What front-end skills does a typical roadmap list for HTML, CSS, and JavaScript?</li>
<li>Define front-end, back-end, and full-stack development. Which does this course cover?</li>
</ol></en-note>]]></content><created>20261002T000625Z</created><updated>20261002T000625Z</updated><tag>LIS467</tag><tag>lecture-notes</tag><note-attributes><author>panopto_transcribe.py</author><source>markdown</source></note-attributes></note><note><title>LIS 467 — Session 3, Part 4: HTML5</title><content><![CDATA[<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE en-note SYSTEM "http://xml.evernote.com/pub/enml2.dtd">
<en-note><h1>LIS 467 — Session 3, Part 4: HTML5</h1>
<p><strong>Course:</strong> LIS 467 — Web Development &amp; Information Architecture
<strong>Topic:</strong> What HTML5 adds, learning resources, CodePen, basic HTML elements and attributes, block vs. inline elements, session wrap-up
<strong>Source:</strong> Panopto lecture transcript — "Session 3 Part 4 HTML5"</p>
<hr />
<h2>1. What Is HTML5?</h2>
<p>🔑 <strong>HTML5</strong> is the current standard for <strong>HTML (HyperText Markup Language)</strong>. It keeps the stable elements and attributes of earlier versions and adds:</p>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">New capability</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Examples</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Multimedia</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Native <code>&lt;video&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;audio&gt;</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Graphics</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><code>&lt;canvas&gt;</code>, SVG</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Storage</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Local storage</strong> in the browser; client-side SQL database</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>⭐ HTML5 is a <strong>big improvement</strong> over previous versions.</p>
<hr />
<h2>2. Learning Resources</h2>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Resource</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Lynda.com → LinkedIn Learning</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Free via Simmons — log in with your Simmons email/credentials for HTML5 and design tutorials</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>W3Schools</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Tutorials and "Try it" examples</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Textbooks</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Many HTML5 texts available</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>3. Editing HTML Locally (Recap)</h2>
<ol>
<li>Write code in <strong>Notepad++</strong> (or another text editor).</li>
<li><strong>Save as <code>.html</code></strong> → color coding appears.</li>
<li>Open the file in a <strong>browser</strong> to view the rendered page.</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>4. CodePen</h2>
<p>🔑 <strong>CodePen</strong> (codepen.io) is an online playground for front-end code. Create a free account, then create a new <strong>Pen</strong>:</p>
<pre><code>┌────────────┬────────────┬────────────┐
│   HTML     │    CSS     │    JS      │
├────────────┴────────────┴────────────┤
│        Live rendered preview         │
└──────────────────────────────────────┘
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li>Paste HTML in one panel, add CSS and JavaScript in the others.</li>
<li>✅ See the result <strong>immediately</strong> — a nice way to <strong>play around with code</strong> and try tutorial examples.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>5. Basic HTML Elements &amp; Attributes</h2>
<h3>Elements and nesting</h3>
<ul>
<li>🔑 An <strong>element</strong> = opening tag + content + closing tag, e.g. <code>&lt;p&gt;Hello&lt;/p&gt;</code></li>
<li>Elements can be <strong>nested</strong> inside other elements.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Attributes</h3>
<p>🔑 <strong>Attributes</strong> provide extra information about an element, written as <code>name="value"</code> inside the opening tag.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.simmons.edu&quot;&gt;Simmons University&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;img src=&quot;library.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Front entrance of the library&quot;&gt;
</code></pre>
<h3>Common elements</h3>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Element</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Purpose</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> … <code>&lt;h6&gt;</code></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Headings</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> most important → <code>&lt;h6&gt;</code> least</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><code>&lt;p&gt;</code></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Paragraph</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><code>&lt;a href="…"&gt;</code></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Anchor / link</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><code>href</code> attribute holds the destination URL</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><code>&lt;img src="…" alt="…"&gt;</code></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Image</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><code>src</code> = image source; ⭐ <strong>always provide <code>alt</code> text</strong> — it helps <strong>accessibility</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><code>target="_blank"</code></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Attribute on <code>&lt;a&gt;</code></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Opens the link in a <strong>new tab</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><code>&lt;div&gt;</code></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Block-level</strong> container</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Starts on a new line, takes full width</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><code>&lt;span&gt;</code></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Inline</strong> container</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Stays within the line of text</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Example</h3>
<pre><code>&lt;h1&gt;My Library&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Welcome to our &lt;span&gt;community&lt;/span&gt; library.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.w3schools.com&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot;&gt;
  &lt;img src=&quot;logo.png&quot; alt=&quot;Library logo&quot;&gt;
&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;This paragraph sits inside a block-level div.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>
<h3>Block vs. inline</h3>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Type</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Behavior</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Examples</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Block-level</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">New line; full available width</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><code>&lt;div&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;p&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Inline</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Flows within text; only as wide as content</td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><code>&lt;span&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;a&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;img&gt;</code></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>💡 Practice these on <strong>W3Schools</strong>, <strong>CodePen</strong>, or other resources.</p>
<hr />
<h2>6. Session 3 Wrap-Up</h2>
<p>This session covered:</p>
<ul>
<li>✅ The importance of <strong>audience</strong> and <strong>personas</strong></li>
<li>✅ <strong>Design considerations</strong> for websites, with good and bad examples</li>
<li>✅ <strong>Web development tools</strong> — including the full stack (front-end + back-end); ⚠️ this course focuses on <strong>client-side / front-end</strong> development</li>
<li>✅ An introduction to <strong>HTML5</strong></li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Key Terms Glossary</h2>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Term</th>
<th style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;background-color:#f0f0f0;text-align:left;">Definition</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>HTML5</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Current HTML standard; adds multimedia, graphics, and storage features</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Element</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Opening tag, content, and closing tag</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Tag</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Markup like <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;/p&gt;</code> defining an element</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Attribute</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Name/value pair in an opening tag giving extra info (e.g., <code>href</code>, <code>src</code>, <code>alt</code>)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong><code>href</code></strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Link destination attribute of <code>&lt;a&gt;</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong><code>src</code></strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Source file attribute of <code>&lt;img&gt;</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong><code>alt</code></strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Alternative text for images; essential for accessibility</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Block-level element</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Starts on a new line and spans full width (<code>&lt;div&gt;</code>)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Inline element</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Flows within a line of text (<code>&lt;span&gt;</code>)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>CodePen</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Online HTML/CSS/JS editor with live preview</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;"><strong>Local storage</strong></td>
<td style="border:1px solid #ccc;padding:4px 8px;">Browser-based client-side data storage introduced with HTML5</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>Quick Review Questions</h2>
<ol>
<li>What new capabilities did HTML5 introduce?</li>
<li>How can Simmons students access LinkedIn Learning tutorials?</li>
<li>What does CodePen provide, and why is it useful for practice?</li>
<li>Write the HTML for a link that opens W3Schools in a new tab.</li>
<li>Why should every <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> have an <code>alt</code> attribute?</li>
<li>What's the difference between <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;span&gt;</code>?</li>
</ol></en-note>]]></content><created>20261002T000625Z</created><updated>20261002T000625Z</updated><tag>LIS467</tag><tag>lecture-notes</tag><note-attributes><author>panopto_transcribe.py</author><source>markdown</source></note-attributes></note></en-export>
